
As part of developing and debugging the Conference Management application, I created a dedicated Python script called test_functions.py. 
This script directly calls each database function (Options 1–6) without going through the interactive menu. 
This addition significantly improved reliability, debugging, and ensured the application behaved exactly as required by the specification.


SQL errors only appeared after navigating through the menu, making debugging slow and repetitive.

Some functions depended on both MySQL and Neo4j, so a single mistake in one query caused the entire menu flow to break.

The VM environment sometimes behaved differently from my local machine, so I needed a fast way to confirm that each function worked correctly on the VM.

The project specification emphasises that the application must run correctly on the VM, so having a fast, repeatable testing method was handy and helpful.

What the test script does:

test_functions.py imports the functions from db.py and executes them one by one, simulating the behaviour of menu options:

Option 1 — View Speakers & Sessions

Option 2 — View Attendees by Company

Option 3 — Add New Attendee

Option 4 — View Connected Attendees

Option 5 — Add Attendee Connection

Option 6 — View Rooms

Each function is executed with known test inputs, and any errors are printed immediately. 
This allowed me to quickly identify:

Incorrect SQL joins

Wrong column names

Missing tables

Neo4j connection issues

Data‑type mismatches

Logic errors in the Python code












